statement:
For a **connected** scheme $X$ with geometric basepoint $\bar x$, its étale fundamental group $\pi_1(X,\bar x)$ is the profinite automorphism group of the fibre functor of finite étale covers of $X$. A *finite continuous $\pi_1$-set* is a finite set with continuous action; a *finite continuous $\pi_1$-module* is a finite abelian group with compatible continuous action. A scheme is *geometrically unibranch* if every strictly henselian local ring has a unique minimal prime (equivalently the local geometric branches are unique); this is a hypothesis of the extension of the finite-type module correspondence to possibly infinite underlying fibre modules.

**Statement (source hypotheses preserved):**
Let $X$ be a connected scheme. Let $\overline{x}$ be a geometric point of $X$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item There is an equivalence of categories
$$
\left\{
\begin{matrix}
\text{finite locally constant}\\
\text{sheaves of sets on }X_\etale
\end{matrix}
\right\}
\longleftrightarrow
\left\{
\begin{matrix}
\text{finite }\pi_1(X, \overline{x})\text{-sets}
\end{matrix}
\right\}
$$
\item There is an equivalence of categories
$$
\left\{
\begin{matrix}
\text{finite locally constant}\\
\text{sheaves of abelian groups on }X_\etale
\end{matrix}
\right\}
\longleftrightarrow
\left\{
\begin{matrix}
\text{finite }\pi_1(X, \overline{x})\text{-modules}
\end{matrix}
\right\}
$$
\item Let $\Lambda$ be a finite ring. There is an equivalence of categories
$$
\left\{
\begin{matrix}
\text{finite type, locally constant}\\
\text{sheaves of }\Lambda\text{-modules on }X_\etale
\end{matrix}
\right\}
\longleftrightarrow
\left\{
\begin{matrix}
\text{finite }\pi_1(X, \overline{x})\text{-modules endowed}\\
\text{with commuting }\Lambda\text{-module structure}
\end{matrix}
\right\}
$$
\end{enumerate}

**Typed interpretation:** The claim is interpreted using the preceding explicit definitions; retain exactly the original assumptions and conclusion.

